#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138314 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138442 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e4b91363-1cac-5495-b29b-c14236bc4de6" :knowledge-point/title "Performing a Unit Conversion: Converting a Numerator"}] :question/id #uuid "638af342-1995-5403-84bd-b6c720cb432d" :question/math-academy-id "e-907" :question/problem "A guitar string, made of carbon steel, has a linear mass density of $3\\,950$ milligrams per meter. What is the linear mass density in grams per meter?" :question/worked-solution "We are given the units\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{mg}}{\\textrm{m}}\n$$\n\nand we want to convert this into\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{m}}\n$$\n\nTo figure out what we need to multiply by, we can set up an equation to help us:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{mg}}{\\textrm{m}}\\cdot\\, \\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{m}}\n$$\n\nWe want to replace $\\textrm{mg}$ with $\\textrm{g}$. To get rid of $\\textrm{mg}$, we can put $\\textrm{mg}$ in the denominator so that it cancels out. To introduce $\\textrm{g}$, we can put $\\textrm{g}$ in the numerator.\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel} \\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{mg}}}{\\textrm{m}}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{mg}}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{m}}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel} \\dfrac{\\textrm{mg}}{\\textrm{m}}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{mg}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{m}}\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use, and apply it.\n\n- The density given in the problem statement is $3\\,950 \\, \\dfrac{\\textrm{mg}}{\\textrm{m}}$.\n- There are $1\\,000$ milligrams per gram. Taking the reciprocal, there are $\\dfrac{1}{1\\,000}$ grams per milligram.\n\nApplying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3950\\frac{mg}{m} &= 3950\\frac{mg}{m} \\cdot \\frac{1}{1000}\\frac{g}{mg} \\\\\n&= (3950 \\cdot \\frac{1}{1000})\\frac{mg}{m} \\cdot \\frac{g}{mg} \\\\\n&= 3.95\\frac{g}{m}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the linear mass density of the string is $3.95\\,\\textrm{g}/\\textrm{m}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138315 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138443 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f10e8464-e555-5358-b97f-230933822fa1" :knowledge-point/title "Performing a Unit Conversion: Converting a Denominator"}] :question/id #uuid "08aa3547-cb51-5d00-8cb7-3e1a9bd4a695" :question/math-academy-id "e-908" :question/problem "Sand runs through a sand timer at a rate of $120$ grams per minute. What is this rate in grams per second?" :question/worked-solution "We are given the units\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\nand we want to convert this into\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nTo figure out what we need to multiply by, we can set up an equation to help us:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{min}}\\cdot\\, \\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nWe want to replace $\\textrm{min}$ with $\\textrm{s}$. To get rid of $\\textrm{min}$, we can put $\\textrm{min}$ in the numerator so that it cancels out. To introduce $\\textrm{s}$, we can put $\\textrm{s}$ in the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel} \\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{min}}}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{min}}}{\\textrm{s}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{min}}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{min}}{\\textrm{s}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use, and apply it.\n\n- The rate given in the problem statement is $120 \\, \\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{min}}$.\n- There are $60$ seconds per minute. Taking the reciprocal, there are $\\dfrac{1}{60}$ minutes per second.\n\nApplying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n120\\frac{g}{min} &= 120\\frac{g}{min} \\cdot \\frac{1}{60}\\frac{min}{s} \\\\\n&= (120 \\cdot \\frac{1}{60})\\frac{g}{min} \\cdot \\frac{min}{s} \\\\\n&= 2\\frac{g}{s}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the sand runs through the sand timer at a rate of $2 \\,\\textrm{g}/\\textrm{s}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138316 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138444 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b82abac-b8b5-5e9e-bac1-38cc4f4de6c0" :knowledge-point/title "Performing a Unit Conversion: Converting a Numerator and a Denominator"}] :question/id #uuid "79f9c66b-2e4e-53ad-8146-10a2aa4e730d" :question/math-academy-id "e-909" :question/problem "A peregrine falcon can reach a speed of $360$ kilometers per hour during flight while diving. What is this speed in meters per second?" :question/worked-solution "We are given the units\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}}{\\textrm{hour}}\n$$\n\nand we want to convert this into\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nWe're converting two units instead of just one, so we're going to have to multiply by two conversion factors:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}}{\\textrm{hour}}\\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} \\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nOne of the conversion factors should change $\\textrm{km}$ to $\\textrm{m}$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\color{blue}\\textrm{km}}}{\\textrm{hour}}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\cancel{\\color{blue}\\textrm{km}}} \\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nThe other conversion factor should change $\\textrm{hour}$ into $\\textrm{s}$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\color{blue}\\textrm{km}}}{\\cancel{\\color{red}\\textrm{hour}}}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\cancel{\\color{blue}\\textrm{km}}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\cancel{\\color{red}\\textrm{hour}}}{\\textrm{s}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{black}\\textrm{km}}{\\color{black}\\textrm{hour}}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\color{black}\\textrm{km}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\color{black}\\textrm{hour}}{\\textrm{s}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use, and apply it.\n\n- The rate given in the problem statement is $360 \\, \\dfrac{\\textrm{km}}{\\textrm{hour}}$.\n- There are $1\\,000$ meters per kilometer.\n- The number of seconds in one hour is given by\n$1hour|= 60minutes; = 60 \\cdot 1minute; = 60 \\cdot 60s; = 3600s$.\nTaking the reciprocal, we find that there are $\\dfrac{1}{3\\,600}$ hours per second.\n\nApplying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n360\\frac{km}{hour} &= 360\\frac{km}{hour} \\cdot 1000\\frac{m}{km} \\cdot \\frac{1}{3600}\\frac{hour}{s} \\\\\n&= (360 \\cdot 1000 \\cdot \\frac{1}{3600})\\frac{km}{hour} \\cdot \\frac{m}{km} \\cdot \\frac{hour}{s} \\\\\n&= 100\\frac{m}{s}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the speed of the falcon is $100$ meters per second."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138828 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138444 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b82abac-b8b5-5e9e-bac1-38cc4f4de6c0" :knowledge-point/title "Performing a Unit Conversion: Converting a Numerator and a Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da8a2177-c88b-5f7a-88f2-fdc3f96a2faa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "175min/week" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186135917} {:answer/id #uuid "3d40755f-6093-59b3-af5b-594701f265c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "250min/week" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186135918} {:answer/id #uuid "3ff121e0-e61f-5006-a604-15adfd4a1e73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "210min/week" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186135919} {:answer/id #uuid "4dab7fb0-225a-501d-b916-845297b00aa4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "160min/week" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186135920} {:answer/id #uuid "70ac10ad-d42f-5790-b4f4-86aa8b501964" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "150min/week" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186135921}] :answer-field/id #uuid "0d620be5-872f-567c-b4eb-13ab7a596c28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186135916}] :question/id #uuid "e4e12086-1e27-5f26-877c-16c074270288" :question/math-academy-id "q-52042" :question/problem "Lewis spends $0.5$ hours per day studying. How many minutes per week does Lewis study for?\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}]}
